    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks (e.g., vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, DeepSpeed-MI/SGLang-style deployments) combine them: dense/attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP (often with TP *within* an expert group), and data parallelism may sit on top for throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What is sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer:\n\n- **Attention**: Query/Key/Value projection weights are column-parallel (each device computes a slice of heads). The output projection `W_O` is row-parallel (each device holds a slice of input features; outputs are partial sums).\n- **MLP**: First GEMM (`W1`, up/gate projections) is column-parallel; second GEMM (`W2`, down projection) is row-parallel.\n- **Embeddings**: Either row-sharded (vocab-parallel, each device owns a vocab range) or replicated.\n- **LayerNorm, biases, KV cache**: Typically replicated (KV cache may also be TP-sharded by head, which is standard since heads are already split).\n\n### 2.2 Data flow per layer\n\nFor an MLP block with input activation `X` (shape `[batch, seq, hidden]`), sharded",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
